40625, 0.00492095947265625, -0.0172882080078125, -0.01377105712890625, 0.0273590087890625, 0.03814697265625, 0.03155517578125, 0.033905029296875, 0.01467132568359375, 0.0172882080078125, 0.0019588470458984375, -0.0292510986328125, 0.00839996337890625, 0.01126861572265625, -0.0002281665802001953, -0.0293426513671875, -0.003597259521484375, 0.0138702392578125, -0.0251007080078125, -0.03460693359375, 0.004299163818359375, -0.00902557373046875, 0.0084381103515625, -0.011077880859375, -0.022430419921875, -0.01433563232421875, 0.00302886962890625, -0.006122589111328125, -0.0231475830078125, 0.0240631103515625, -0.0167236328125, 0.0286712646484375, 0.01080322265625, -0.043121337890625, -0.011749267578125, 0.01430511474609375, 0.010833740234375, -0.032501220703125, 0.0144500732421875, 0.016937255859375, -0.01198577880859375, 0.020294189453125, -0.0204925537109375, -0.02001953125, 0.0262451171875, -0.01042938232421875, 0.00907135009765625, 0.004058837890625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent research has begun to investigate the use of Geometric Algebra (GA) in the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems\u2014most notably in the study of electrical circuits (see, e.g., [1], [2], [3]). In analysis and design, transforming the ordinary differential equations (ODEs) that describe system behavior from the time domain to the Laplace domain is foundational. While Laplace transforms are well established in real and complex analysis and have been extended to select hypercomplex algebras such as quaternions, dual numbers, and Clifford algebras [4], [5], these extensions often treat only particular subclasses of hypercomplex algebras, typically with a specific focus on their scalar or simple vector components. Consequently, prior work does not generally address the challenges posed by the full multivector structure and rich grading of general geometric algebras as required in broad GA-based modeling.\n\nThe specific problem addressed in this work is the lack of a rigorous, comprehensive definition and framework for the Laplace transform in general geometric algebras, explicitly geared toward handling arbitrary multivector-valued functions relevant in continuous-time dynamical systems. The primary objectives are (1) to provide a constructive and generalizable definition of the Laplace transform suited for GA, (2) to establish essential properties (such as linearity, differentiation, and invertibility) in this new setting, and (3) to clarify the implications for system analysis, especially in GA-based descriptions of electrical and physical systems.\n\nA key novelty of our contribution is that, in contrast to prior extensions (e.g., [4], [5]) that are limited in algebraic scope or fail to encompass the full range of multivector grades, our approach accommodates arbitrary multivectors in geometric algebras and systematically addresses their algebraic interactions when transformed to the Laplace domain. Previous approaches often assume commutative multiplication or restrict analysis to low-dimensional or purely scalar components, whereas our treatment explicitly retains the noncommutative and graded structure of GA, ensuring the transform is both mathematically sound and physically meaningful for GA-modeled systems.\n\nOur results are immediately applicable to continuous-time systems encountered in typical physics and engineering settings, including (but not limited to) RLC electrical circuits represented in three-dimensional GA (signature (3,0)), spacetime electrodynamics in GA with signature (1,3), and spatial kinematics in robotics. The main outcome is a set of foundational definitions and theorems supporting the Laplace transform for such systems, supported by proofs and illustrative computational examples showing its concrete use in, for instance, Laplace domain analysis of circuits with multivector-valued currents and voltages.\n\nThe scope of our investigation is presently limited to geometric algebras with signature less than or equal to 5. This boundary arises from substantive increases in dimension-dependent computational complexity and algebraic subtleties as the signature increases. Specifically, higher dimensional algebras introduce more intricate grade structures and an exponential rise in basis elements, making explicit calculation and even representation of the Laplace transform more cumbersome. Moreover, ensuring that transformed multivector functions admit unique and invertible Laplace-domain (i.e., frequency domain) representations\u2014which means that each original time-domain multivector function can be faithfully mapped to and recovered from its Laplace-domain counterpart\u2014requires careful attention to algebraic identities, invertibility of multivector-valued functions, and the convergence of componentwise integrals. These issues are amplified for large signatures due to the complex interaction among multivector grades. For foundational work on the properties and challenges associated with computation in higher dimensional geometric algebras, see, for example, [1], [3].\n\nIn summary, our main contributions are:\n- A formal and constructive definition of the Laplace transform for general multivector-valued functions in geometric algebras of signature \u22645;\n- Proofs of essential properties (including linearity, differential properties, and invertibility) supporting robust system analysis;\n- Clarification of the transform's immediate applicability to physical models such as electrical circuits and kinematic systems using geometric algebra;\n- Analysis of algebraic and computational challenges associated with higher signature algebras, motivating the focus on dimensions most relevant for current engineering and physical applications.\n\n[1] Doran, C., & Lasenby, A. (2003). Geometric Algebra for Physicists. Cambridge University Press.\n[2] Hitzer, E., & Sangwine, S. J. (Eds.). (2013). Quaternion and Clifford Fourier Transforms and Wavelets. Springer.\n[3] Lasenby, J., Lasenby, A., & Doran, C. (2000). A Unified Mathematical Language for Physics and Engineering in the 21st Century. Phil. Trans. R. Soc. Lond. A 358, 21-39.\n[4] Kou, K. I., & Liu, L. (2007). Quaternionic Laplace transforms and their applications. Mathematical Methods in the Applied Sciences, 30(15), 1785\u20131797.\n[5] Sobczyk, G. (1995). The Hyperbolic Number Plane. The College Mathematics Journal, 26(4), 268\u2013280."}}